# Line endings, decided by the repository rather than by whoever cloned it.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# --------------------
# core.autocrlf=true on a Windows checkout rewrites LF to CRLF on the way out
# of git. For most files that is harmless. For a shell script it is fatal and
# the error does not name the cause:
#
#     verify.sh: line 22: $'\r': command not found
#     verify.sh: line 36: syntax error near unexpected token `$'{\r''
#
# bash reads the carriage return as part of the command. entrypoint.sh is the
# container ENTRYPOINT and verify.sh is the gate that runs before the bot is
# allowed to trade, so a fresh clone on Windows breaks both -- locally, and in
# any image built from that clone.
#
# Render is unaffected, because git stores these blobs with LF and a Linux
# checkout writes them back unchanged. That is exactly what makes it worth
# pinning: the failure appears only on the machine doing the work, which is
# the machine least likely to be blamed.

# Normalise every text file to LF in the repository. Working-copy endings are
# still per-platform for anything not pinned below, so this changes what is
# stored, not what Windows editors see.
* text=auto

# These must be LF in the working copy too, not merely in storage.
*.sh text eol=lf
entrypoint.sh text eol=lf
verify.sh text eol=lf
checkup.sh text eol=lf

# Docker's parser is not a shell, but a line continuation followed by CRLF is
# still a way to get a build failure that reads as a syntax error in a file
# nobody edited. The COPY here spans two lines, so this is not theoretical.
Dockerfile text eol=lf

# YAML is whitespace-significant and Render parses it server-side.
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf

# The cached klines are binary as far as git is concerned; they are gitignored
# anyway, and this makes sure a stray `git add -f` cannot corrupt one.
*.csv -text
